<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hodeidah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hodeidah is a port city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a> coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, in the coastal plain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tihama">Tihama</a>, and the country's main port for imports. It grew in the 19th century under Ottoman rule, when it replaced Mocha as the chief port of the region. Held by the Idrisid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asir">Asir</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, it was taken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yahya-muhammad-hamid-ed-din">Imam Yahya</a>'s Mutawakkilite Kingdom in 1925.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hodeidah.jpg" alt="Hodeidah" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rod Waddington from Kergunyah, Australia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al_Hudaydah_Market,_Yemen_(11042765095).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
